Chapter: Builtins
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The builtins, once. A builtin name used to appear in three hand-maintained
 lists that agreed only by inspection, and each way of disagreeing failed
 differently and at its own distance from the cause: absent from the
 resolver was CDX3002, which kills not just the call but every chapter
 downstream of the one that made it; routed with no emitter was CDX2042 at
 emit. A build-time guard reconciled them, which is not the same as removing
 the disagreement -- it only meant a disagreement could not ship.

 All three are here now, one row each. `builtin-names` is the names in it;
 `builtin-type-env` is a fold of `env-bind` over the ones that carry a type;
 `x86-builtin-emitters` is the rows whose `bs-emit` is a `Just`, and the
 routing list is derived from that in turn, so being routed IS having an
 emitter. None of the three is written out by hand any more, so none of
 them can drift from this one. The build-time guard that used to reconcile
 the emitter column with the rest had nothing left to compare and is
 deleted.

 A row's emitter is a `Maybe` because most names do not have one. 143 of
 the 244 carry a `Just`; the other 101 are effect ops, kernel syscalls and
 constructors reached by paths other than `emit-builtin`, and `None` is the
 honest answer for them rather than a stub that would route them here and
 then fail.

 The emitter is an Emit-quire function, and this chapter names it without
 citing anything. That is what the concatenated unit allows -- Emit sorts
 before Types, so these are backward references -- and the reverse
 direction is ordinary too: `boot-cap-mask-for` in the X86-64 Chapter
 reads `cap-bits-for-name` out of the foreword, and `capability-vocabulary`
 here in Types is a projection of that same table.

 Eight names carry no type, and each is deliberate. `True`, `False` and
 `Nothing` are special-cased by the checker -- that is how CDX2086 gets to
 say that Nothing is a type and not a value. `open-file`, `close-file`,
 `read-all`, `now` and `random-integer` are effect operations, typed by
 their `effect` declarations rather than here. A name with no type resolves
 and is not bound; that is what `None` means in this table, and it is a
 statement rather than an omission.

 The order is the order the let-chain bound in, and it is load-bearing:
 `env-bind` REPLACES, so where a name appears twice the LAST one wins.
 No name appears twice now. `process-exit` did, with two different types,
 and the nullary one bound second and won -- so `process-exit 0` answered
 CDX2001, Integer vs Fun, and the unary form every transpiler emitter
 implements was unreachable. The fix is two
 names for what were always two operations:

 `process-exit` is the host form, `Integer -> [Process] Nothing`. It is
 what `std::process::exit`, `Environment.Exit` and `sys.exit` mean, and
 the 53 plugs emit it under this name.

 `process-exit-self` is the bare-metal form and takes no argument. It is
 the syscall `emit-process-exit-helper` writes: mark this slot FREE, walk
 the wake loop marking every waiter READY, jump to `__idle_dispatch`.
 Its neighbours `process-yield`, `process-wait` and `get-ticks` are the
 same nullary shape, which is the family it belongs to.

 The unary form has an x86 emitter too: a
 process-table exit-status cell, written by `process-exit`'s helper and
 by `__proc_entry` from the closure's return value, delivered to a
 waiter through its saved RAX by the exit wake loop, and answered by
 `process-wait` -- so a child's exit status means the same thing on
 bare metal as on the hosts.

Section: The Table
  BuiltinSpec = record {
   bs-name : Text,
   bs-type : Maybe CodexType,
   bs-emit : Maybe (CodegenState, List IRExpr -> EmitResult)
  }

 The four session-channel type names were `let` bindings inside the chain,
 which a table cannot hold. They are definitions now, so the type
 expressions below are the ones that were there, unedited.

  ssend-name : Name = Name { value = "SSend" }
  srecv-name : Name = Name { value = "SRecv" }
  send-name : Name = Name { value = "SEnd" }
  schan-name : Name = Name { value = "SChan" }

